{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2969","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2969","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2969\",\"parent_revision\":\"3dd9a8a683143dceb7abaf5f955bee13f233c358\",\"root_revision\":\"7fdc9d2455d3217ace4383984516d4bebc080e24600c577a2672c24ef2f3e951\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-18\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nAudit requested while reworking #2956 (PR #2964): check content_normalizer.py, disclosure_handler.py, and ordinal_mapper.py for the same class of bug #2956 was -- a transform that should be single-source-of-truth but has grown a second, independently-written copy elsewhere, with nothing enforcing the two agree. Two findings, both real architectural risk; neither confirmed as a currently-live bug, and neither is a cheap one-line fix, so both are filed rather than fixed inline.\\n\\n## Finding 1: \\\"## Sections [N] Title (M entries)\\\" index format has 3 independent generators, 1 independent parser\\n\\nGenerators (each independently hand-writes the \\\"[N] Title (M entries/subsections)\\\" line format):\\n- backlog_core/operations.py _build_sections_index_from_body() (~line 1788) -- from raw body text\\n- backlog_core/operations.py _render_section_index() (~line 1818) -- from a BacklogItem's structured sections\\n- backlog_core/server.py _sections_index_from_result() (~line 2295) -- from a ViewItemResult.sections dict\\n\\nParser (the only consumer that reads this format back into structured data):\\n- backlog_core/content_normalizer.py _parse_section_titles() -- splits on \\\"] \\\" and rsplit(\\\" (\\\", 1) to recover titles\\n\\nNo canonical format function exists that all 3 generators delegate to, and nothing tests that the parser correctly round-trips output from all 3 generators. A future change to any one generator's exact wording/spacing (e.g. \\\"subsections\\\" vs \\\"entries\\\", bracket format) could silently break the parser for that one path without a test catching it. Not yet confirmed to currently diverge -- would need a byte-level comparison across all 3 generators' output plus targeted round-trip tests to confirm live vs. latent.\\n\\n## Finding 2: Ordinal-path format has 1 independent regex validator, several independent string-formatting generators\\n\\nGenerator (produces ordinal path strings via plain f-string composition):\\n- backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py _entry_ordinal_for_sub_heading() -> f\\\"{parent}.{idx}\\\"\\n- backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py _entry_ordinal_for_code() -> f\\\"{parent}.code.{k}\\\"\\n\\nValidator (independently hand-written regex describing the same shape):\\n- backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py _ORDINAL_PATTERN = re.compile(r\\\"^\\\\d+(\\\\.\\\\d+)*(\\\\.code\\\\.\\\\d+)?$\\\")\\n\\nInspection did not find a live mismatch (the regex currently accepts every shape the generator functions can produce), but the regex is not derived from or tested against the generator -- a change to either side could silently desynchronize them, same risk class as #2956's fixed _normalize_section_key/parse_issue_body mismatch.\\n\\n**Research first**: for Finding 1, consider extracting one canonical render_section_index_line(idx, title, count, is_groomed) function all 3 generators call, and a matching parse_section_index_line() the parser uses -- mirroring the rendering.heading_to_unknown_key single-source-of-truth pattern #2956 established. For Finding 2, add a hypothesis property test asserting every ordinal string ordinal_mapper.py can generate matches _ORDINAL_PATTERN (cheaper than a full refactor, and would have caught #2956-shaped drift automatically). Confirm current behavior with fresh reads of all 3 generators' outputs before choosing an approach.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **duplicate-independent-implementation audit: 'wire format' contracts (section-index lines, ordinal paths) have multiple hand-written generators/validators with no shared single source of truth** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Agent task - auto-derived from input, audit requested while reworking #2956/PR #2964\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-18\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Investigation Update 2026 08 20\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRe-audited fresh against current `main` (post-#3040). Confirmed the issue's own inventory is complete \u2014 a broad grep across `backlog_core/*.py`, `sam_schema/**/*.py`, and `scripts/*.py` found no additional independent generators/validators beyond the two findings already named.\\n\\n**Finding 1 (section-index line format)** \u2014 confirmed genuinely duplicated: 3 generators (`operations.py::_build_sections_index_from_body()`, `operations.py::_render_section_index()`, `server.py::_sections_index_from_result()`) each hand-write the `\\\"[N] Title (M entries/subsections)\\\"` line independently, with 1 parser (`content_normalizer.py::_parse_section_titles()`) that reads it back. New evidence beyond the issue text: the three generators' output text *already diverges today* for groomed sections \u2014 `_render_section_index`/`_sections_index_from_result` branch on section type (\\\"subsections\\\" vs \\\"entries\\\" vs no parenthetical), while `_build_sections_index_from_body` always emits \\\"entries\\\" regardless of type, since it derives from raw-body parsing with no concept of `GroomedData`. Not currently a live bug only because the parser discards the entire parenthetical via `rsplit(\\\" (\\\", 1)` and never inspects the word choice \u2014 but a divergence in bracket/spacing format itself would break it silently. **Needs a design decision** (exact canonical `render_section_index_line()`/`parse_section_index_line()` signature, how `is_groomed`/count-type threads through 3 different input shapes) \u2014 touches `operations.py` (2 call sites), `server.py`, `content_normalizer.py`. Deferring this to a separate planned session per this repo's Proactive Fix Gate.\\n\\n**Finding 2 (ordinal-path format)** \u2014 confirmed genuinely duplicated but no live mismatch: 2 generators (`ordinal_mapper.py::_entry_ordinal_for_sub_heading()`, `::_entry_ordinal_for_code()`) vs. 1 independent regex validator (`disclosure_handler.py::_ORDINAL_PATTERN`). The regex is currently a permissive superset of everything the generators can produce. Trivial, single-file fix (the hypothesis property test the issue itself specifies, asserting every generator output matches the pattern) \u2014 fixing now in a follow-up PR since it needs no design decision.\\n\\nLeaving this issue open \u2014 Finding 1 still needs the design decision above before it can be closed; Finding 2 will be referenced by its own PR but doesn't fully close this issue on its own.\\n\\n\\n**Finding 2 fix up for review**: PR #3045 \u2014 hypothesis property test asserting `ordinal_mapper.py`'s generators always match `disclosure_handler._ORDINAL_PATTERN`. Confirms no live mismatch exists today. Finding 1 (section-index-line format) remains open pending the design decision noted above \u2014 not closing this issue until that's resolved too.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Finding 2 Ordinal Path Format Has 1 Independent Regex Validator Several Independent String Formatting Generators\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nGenerator (produces ordinal path strings via plain f-string composition):\\n- backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py _entry_ordinal_for_sub_heading() -> f\\\"{parent}.{idx}\\\"\\n- backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py _entry_ordinal_for_code() -> f\\\"{parent}.code.{k}\\\"\\n\\nValidator (in